    <h1>Highbanker vs <em>Wash Plant</em>: When Do You Scale Up?</h1>
    <p>Both machines wash gravel and catch gold in a sluice. The difference is who feeds them, and that one difference decides which belongs on your claim.</p>
    <p style="font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);margin-top:16px">Published {{ datePublished | readableDate }} · Gold Wash Plants</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container">
    <article class="content">
      <p>A highbanker is fed by hand, one shovel at a time. A wash plant is fed by an excavator, and it is built to keep up with one. Everything else follows from that.</p>

      <p>So the honest answer to "should I upgrade" is not about the machines at all. If you are still finding out whether your ground carries gold, a highbanker is the right tool and a wash plant is an expensive mistake. If you already know the ground pays and your daily total is set by how fast you can move a shovel, you have outgrown the highbanker.</p>

      <h2>What a Highbanker Is Built For</h2>

      <p>A highbanker sits up on the bank above the water line. A pump pushes water from a creek or a tub through the header box, and you shovel or bucket material into the hopper. It is light enough to carry to ground a truck cannot reach, and one or two people can run it.</p>

      <p>That design is a real advantage when you are prospecting. You can test four spots on a claim in a weekend, carry it in on your back where access is bad, and find out what the ground actually holds before you commit money to it. Our <a href="/placer-mining-equipment/">placer mining equipment guide</a> puts highbankers and sluices in the sampling category for exactly that reason.</p>

      <p>The limit is not the sluice. It is the shovel in front of it.</p>

      <h2>Four Signs You Have Outgrown It</h2>

      <p><strong>You are shoveling all day and the pile has not moved.</strong> When the machine sits idle waiting on you, buying a better highbanker does not fix anything. You are already at the ceiling of hand feeding.</p>

      <p><strong>You have already proved the ground.</strong> Sampling has a finish line. Once you know the grade across the deposit, more sampling is not information, it is just slower mining. If you have not got there yet, start with <a href="/blog/testing-gold-deposit-before-buying-equipment/">testing your deposit before you buy equipment</a>.</p>

      <p><strong>Your feed is uneven.</strong> A sluice recovers best on steady, classified feed. Hand shoveling is surges and pauses by nature, and a surge pushes material across the riffles faster than fine gold can settle out of it. The finer your gold, the more that costs you. Our guide to <a href="/blog/fine-gold-recovery/">fine gold recovery</a> covers why.</p>

      <p><strong>You are hiring people to shovel.</strong> This is the clearest signal. At that point you are paying wages to buy throughput, which is the one thing a machine sells cheaper than a person does.</p>

      <h2>What Changes With a Wash Plant</h2>

      <p>Our wash plants are all-in-one systems. Screening, classification and gold recovery happen in a single portable unit, so you are not buying a screen, a trommel and a sluice box and then making them work together. You connect water, feed material, and run.</p>

      <p>Three things change immediately. Feeding moves from a shovel to an excavator or loader, so throughput stops depending on how tired you are. Recovery runs at 95 to 98 percent including fine gold down to 200 mesh. And the whole system is mercury-free, using only water and gravity, which keeps you clear of the regulatory problem that follows mercury recovery everywhere.</p>

      <p>Crew size does not go up. One person can run the system, with a second in the excavator feeding it. Many operations run on one or two people.</p>

      <h2>What the First Production Plant Looks Like</h2>

      <p>For most people stepping up from a highbanker, that is our <a href="/products/50-ton/">50-ton plant</a>. It processes 50 tons per hour through roughly 15 feet of sluice, swallows rock up to 13 inches, and runs on diesel or electric.</p>

      <p>Setup is about 30 minutes. Level it, connect your water, start feeding. Build time is one to three weeks depending on how it is configured, and every plant is built in the USA to the ground it will run on, which is why we quote rather than publish a list price.</p>

      <p>If 50 tons per hour sounds like more than your claim needs, or less, read <a href="/blog/choosing-right-size-gold-wash-plant/">what size wash plant do I need</a> before you decide. Buying too big is as expensive a mistake as buying too small.</p>

      <h2>What You Need On Site First</h2>

      <p>Three things, and none of them is a river. You need water, a way to feed the plant, and fuel.</p>

      <p>On water, the number that scares people is the flow rate, not the consumption. About 500 GPM moves through a 50-ton plant, but with a recirculating settling pond your actual consumption is closer to 50 to 75 GPM. That is the water lost to evaporation and to moisture leaving with the tailings, not water you have to supply continuously. The full breakdown is in our guide to <a href="/blog/gold-wash-plant-water-requirements/">wash plant water requirements</a>.</p>

      <p>For feeding, an excavator or loader. For fuel, whatever the engine takes. That is the site.</p>

      <h2>Keep the Highbanker</h2>

      <p>Scaling up does not make the highbanker useless. It makes it the sampling tool again, which is the job it was always best at. The next claim you look at still needs testing before you move a production plant onto it, and a highbanker is still the cheapest way to get that answer.</p>

      <p>Before you commit either way, run the numbers on what your ground actually carries. <a href="/blog/gold-per-yard-profitability/">How much gold per yard do you need to be profitable</a> walks through the break-even math, and it is the calculation that should drive this decision rather than the size of the machine.</p>

